    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Ming dynasty restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> rule in 1368 after driving the Mongol Yuan court back to the steppe.</li><li>Its examination-based bureaucracy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s voyages and global <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> trade made it a centre of the early modern world.</li><li>Fiscal crisis, famine and rebellion let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644, opening the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manchus">Manchu</a> Qing.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> seen by visitors today is largely a 16th-century brick-faced work, built after the Zhengtong Emperor was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oirats">Oirats</a> at Tumu in 1449.</p></aside>
    </section>

