    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hongwu-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor.jpg" alt="Hongwu Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_assis_de_l%27empereur_Ming_Taizu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ming Empire was founded in 1368 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, a former peasant and Buddhist novice who rose to prominence during the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. The Mongol-led Yuan had suffered internal strife, fiscal mismanagement, devastating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yellow-river">Yellow River</a> floods and widespread uprisings, allowing regional warlords and rebel leaders to seize power. Emerging from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a> (c. 1351–1368), Zhu took Nanjing in 1356 and unified southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> by defeating his rivals, above all Chen Youliang at the great naval battle of Lake Poyang in 1363 and Zhang Shicheng in 1367. In January 1368 he proclaimed the Ming dynasty at Nanjing, reigning as the Hongwu Emperor, and later that year his armies captured the Yuan capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dadu">Dadu</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beijing">Beijing</a>), driving the Mongol court back to the steppe. He launched a programme of centralisation, Confucian ideological renewal and territorial consolidation. The dynasty's early foundations rested on disciplined military organisation, administrative restructuring and the reassertion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> traditions as a source of legitimacy. Control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Yellow River basins, combined with the support of key regional elites, enabled the Ming to stabilise China and begin a period of growth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#manila"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manila.jpg" alt="Manila" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#manila">Manila</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cityscape_of_Manila,_2025_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bahnfrend, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ming social and economic order combined continuity with earlier dynasties and innovations in administration. Society was organised hierarchically, with the emperor at the apex, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a> recruited through the civil service examinations, the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gentry">gentry</a>, peasants and artisans, while merchants held an ambiguous status despite their wealth. Agriculture formed the backbone of the economy, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a> supplemented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sericulture">sericulture</a> and, from the late 16th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#columbian-exchange">New World crops</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>. The state compiled land surveys (the fish-scale registers) and population registers (the yellow registers) and organised households under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lijia-system">lijia</a> system to assess taxes and labour service. Domestic markets thrived, and in the early 15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s voyages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a>. Revenue came from taxes in kind and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, consolidated in the Single Whip reforms of the 16th century, and from the salt monopoly. Silver imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a> and, via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila">Manila</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spanish-america">Spanish America</a> monetised the economy, fuelling population growth, urbanisation and commercial expansion, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> delta.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#yongle-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor.jpg" alt="Yongle Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_assis_de_l%27empereur_Ming_Chengzu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ming military evolved into a large and highly organised system combining infantry, artillery and cavalry. The early Ming adopted some Mongol practices but organised its forces into hereditary military households under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#weisuo-system">Weisuo system</a> of garrisons, which was meant to make soldier-farmers self-supporting. Garrison towns along the northern frontier, and the long brick-faced walls built mainly in the 16th century and now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>, reflected a growing emphasis on defence, especially after the Zhengtong Emperor was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oirats">Oirat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> at Tumu in 1449. Infantry used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crossbow">crossbows</a>, pikes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">cannon</a> and firearms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hand-cannon">hand cannons</a>, while cavalry provided mobility and rapid response. Notable campaigns included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a>'s five expeditions into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongolia">Mongolia</a>, the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a> (1407–1427), the suppression of coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#piracy">pirates</a> (wokou) by commanders such as Qi Jiguang, and the intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a> against the Japanese invasions of 1592–1598. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s fleets also projected Ming naval power across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. Military strength underpinned internal stability and foreign influence, allowing the Ming to govern a vast and diverse empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#grand-secretariat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-secretariat.jpg" alt="Grand Secretariat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#grand-secretariat">Grand Secretariat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wen_Yuan_Chamber.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gisling, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Government in the Ming Empire was highly centralised under the emperor, supported by an extensive bureaucracy shaped by Confucian principles. After abolishing the office of chancellor in 1380, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> ruled directly through the Six Ministries, and under his successors a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grand-secretariat">Grand Secretariat</a> developed to assist the throne, while palace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eunuch">eunuchs</a> gained increasing influence. Officials selected through the civil service examinations administered provinces, prefectures and counties, giving government a meritocratic character. The state maintained detailed population and tax registers, and the law was codified in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-ming-code">Great Ming Code</a> (Da Ming Lü). Central ministries coordinated military, fiscal and ritual affairs, while a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#censorate">Censorate</a> monitored the conduct of officials. The emperor retained supreme authority and could override bureaucratic decisions, yet the administrative structure secured relative uniformity in law and resource allocation. Justice combined statutory law with Confucian norms. This balance of central authority and bureaucratic professionalism enabled the Ming state to mobilise resources efficiently for defence and infrastructure across a territory of some 6 million square kilometres at its height.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#beijing"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beijing.jpg" alt="Beijing" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#beijing">Beijing</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Beijing_CBD_with_B-5906_approaching_(20211016171955)_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N509FZ, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ming Empire exerted profound influence within East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> and beyond during the early modern period. Domestically, the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> rule reinforced Confucian social order and cultural continuity. Economically, the circulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a> integrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> with East Asian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and, from the 16th century, global trade networks, with Ming blue-and-white porcelain prized from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zheng-he">Zheng He</a>'s expeditions (1405–1433) extended China's diplomatic and commercial reach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-asia">South Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a>, establishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tributary-system">tributary relations</a> and projecting imperial prestige. Ming relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a> and Central Asian states shaped regional alliances, trade and cultural exchange. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beijing">Beijing</a>, where the Forbidden City was completed in 1420, restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> to supply it, and sponsored the vast Yongle Encyclopedia. The Ming legacy persisted in Chinese government, in Neo-Confucian thought such as that of Wang Yangming, in vernacular novels and the arts, and in the institutions of successor dynasties and neighbouring states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nurhaci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nurhaci.jpg" alt="Nurhaci" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nurhaci">Nurhaci</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%B8%85_%E4%BD%9A%E5%90%8D_%E3%80%8A%E6%B8%85%E5%A4%AA%E7%A5%96%E5%A4%A9%E5%91%BD%E7%9A%87%E5%B8%9D%E6%9C%9D%E6%9C%8D%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B_1361x1869.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Ming Empire was driven by internal mismanagement, fiscal strain and external pressure. By the late 16th century, the long withdrawal of the Wanli Emperor from government, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eunuch">eunuch</a> influence and factional struggles such as those between the Donglin faction and its opponents undermined the bureaucracy, while the costs of border defence and of the Korean war strained state finances. Cold weather associated with the Little Ice Age brought repeated droughts, floods and famines, and a fall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> imports in the 1630s deepened the crisis, fuelling peasant uprisings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#li-zicheng-rebellion">Li Zicheng rebellion</a> and that of Zhang Xianzhong. Meanwhile the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manchus">Manchus</a>, united under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, pressed on the north-east frontier. In April 1644 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beijing">Beijing</a> and the Chongzhen Emperor took his own life; the Ming general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> then allied with the Manchus, who defeated Li at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a> and occupied Beijing, establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> there. Ming loyalists in the south formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southern-ming">Southern Ming</a> regimes, which survived in fragmented form until 1662. Ming administrative, cultural and technological achievements nevertheless continued to shape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> long after the dynasty's fall.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ming Empire stands as the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> rule after the Mongol Yuan, whose Confucian bureaucracy, northern defences, and the voyages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zheng-he">Zheng He</a> shaped East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> long after the dynasty fell to rebellion and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manchus">Manchu</a> capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beijing">Beijing</a> in 1644.</em></p>

